<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/18th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Battle</p><h1 class="enc-title">Battle of Karnal</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1739">1739</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> · Also known as Karnal · Part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nader-shahs-invasion-of-india">Nader Shah's invasion of India</a> · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Battle of Karnal was fought in 1739 north of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delhi">Delhi</a> between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nader-shah">Nader Shah</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a> and the Mughal emperor Muhammad Shah. Nader's army won a decisive victory, and he went on to occupy and plunder Delhi, taking the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peacock-throne">Peacock Throne</a>. The campaign brought great wealth to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afsharid-empire">Afsharid Empire</a> and exposed Mughal weakness.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-karnal.jpg" alt="Battle of Karnal" width="320" height="281" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Amir Pashaei · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:A_painting_in_Chehel_Sotoun2.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>News that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kabul">Kabul</a> had fallen in June 1738 reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delhi">Delhi</a> in early July, yet the Mughal court did little for months. Only on 2 December 1738 did Muhammad Shah send his leading nobles, the wazir Qamar-ud-Din Khan, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asaf-jah-i">Nizam-ul-Mulk</a> and Khan Dauran, against the invader, and they spent a month camped outside Delhi. Appeals to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajputs">Rajput</a> chiefs, among them <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jai-singh-ii">Sawai Jai Singh</a>, and to the Peshwa Baji Rao brought no help in time. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nader-shah">Nader Shah</a> crossed the river at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/attock">Attock</a> in January 1739. Rustam Ali put his army at Karnal at about 55,000 horsemen, in a camp of some 160,000 people.</p><p>The emperor dug in behind a canal built by Ali Mardan Khan, near Karnal, 110 km north of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delhi">Delhi</a>. Rather than attack this position, Nader moved east of the town towards the Jamuna and threatened to cut the road to Delhi through Panipat, forcing the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mughal-empire">Mughals</a> to come out or be trapped.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>Saadat Khan, who had marched from his province of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/awadh">Oudh</a> with 20,000 cavalry, reached the Mughal camp at midnight, but his baggage train trailed far behind, unguarded, and Persian scouts found it. When the Mughal army came out of its lines, Nader's forces defeated it within three hours. Estimates of the Mughal dead ran up to 30,000, most contemporaries agreeing on about 20,000 and the historian Axworthy suggesting about 10,000, among them some 400 officers and many of the army's best leaders. Recent scholarship puts Persian losses at about 1,100, including 400 killed. Outdated Mughal cannon, elephants that made easy targets and better Persian musketry decided the day.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asaf-jah-i">Nizam-ul-Mulk</a> negotiated with Nader, who demanded an indemnity, and Muhammad Shah then came to the Persian camp in state. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delhi">Delhi</a> a rumour that Nader had been murdered led a crowd to kill five Persian soldiers at a granary, and an uprising followed. Nader's troops answered with a mass killing: men were beheaded by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yamuna">Yamuna</a>, houses plundered and burned, and many people killed themselves and their families rather than fall into their hands. The campaign left the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mughal-empire">Mughal dynasty</a> critically weakened and showed the British East India Company how frail the empire had become.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Battle of Karnal” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Saadat Khan mobilised Oudh in 1739 to defend against Nader Shah's invasion of India and was captured at the <b>Battle of Karnal</b>; he tried to negotiate with Nader Shah but died in Delhi.</blockquote><a href="/empire/awadh-state">Awadh State · Military</a></li></ul></section>
